1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,825
This is the multi-doser chain carousel, version eleven.

2
00:00:03,825 --> 00:00:08,632
Thirteen auger dosing modules ride on two roller chains in a vertical loop.

3
00:00:08,632 --> 00:00:16,964
One station, inside the loop, takes each module in turn, tilts it, and doses straight down into a beaker on an analytical balance.

4
00:00:16,964 --> 00:00:33,243
Version eleven follows the design notes of November twenty-fifth: the augers tilt all the way to ninety degrees, the keyed rail no longer lifts the module, the electronics carriage opens each outlet door itself, and the balance keeps its tall draft cage.

5
00:00:33,243 --> 00:00:40,100
Nothing has been built yet, so every number in this video is a prediction from the CAD and the simulations.

6
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,681
The frame is twenty-twenty aluminium extrusion with quarter-inch aluminium side plates, tied together by ladder rungs.

7
00:00:48,681 --> 00:00:56,556
Four corner shafts each carry a pair of thirty-two tooth sprockets, so the front and back chains can't drift out of step.

8
00:00:56,556 --> 00:01:08,922
The chains are Nitro Chain's number thirty-five roller chain, about twenty-four dollars for a ten-foot roll, and every eighth link is an A-1 attachment link that a printed carriage bolts to.

9
00:01:08,922 --> 00:01:13,608
A NEMA thirty-four stepper drives one shaft through a short drive chain.

10
00:01:14,508 --> 00:01:20,732
Each module is the lab's mounting plate, carried on to the auger's cap so it supports the whole tube.

11
00:01:20,732 --> 00:01:24,861
The auger snaps into two clips and rests on a saddle under the cap.

12
00:01:24,861 --> 00:01:29,729
A printed T-bracket under the plate is the key that the station's rail catches.

13
00:01:29,729 --> 00:01:37,679
A spring-loaded shutter covers the three millimetre outlet, with a stainless rod up its spring that the station pulls to open it.

14
00:01:37,679 --> 00:01:46,367
The plate hangs on two hinge pins on its carriage's outer face, and a spring latch at its back end holds it, even upside down on the top run.

15
00:01:47,267 --> 00:01:49,528
Building it takes twelve steps.

16
00:01:49,528 --> 00:01:54,997
One: the base frame, a U of extrusion round the balance, on levelling feet.

17
00:01:54,997 --> 00:01:58,716
Two: the posts, the top frame and the ladder rungs.

18
00:01:58,716 --> 00:02:04,039
Three: the aluminium side plates, the front one with the actuator's slot.

19
00:02:04,039 --> 00:02:08,195
Four: the flange bearings and the top shafts' tensioners.

20
00:02:08,195 --> 00:02:11,841
Five: the four corner shafts with their sprockets.

21
00:02:11,841 --> 00:02:15,195
Six: the NEMA thirty-four and its drive chain.

22
00:02:15,195 --> 00:02:19,498
Seven: the two chains, each with thirteen attachment links.

23
00:02:19,498 --> 00:02:25,842
Eight: the station, with its beam, guide, electronics carriage, actuator and latch cam.

24
00:02:25,842 --> 00:02:28,613
Nine: each module, built on the bench.

25
00:02:28,613 --> 00:02:32,696
Ten: the carriages onto the chains, one index at a time.

26
00:02:32,696 --> 00:02:34,884
Eleven: the electronics board.

27
00:02:34,884 --> 00:02:40,353
And twelve: the balance, through the front doorway, and the acrylic panels.

28
00:02:41,253 --> 00:02:45,920
To dose, the NEMA thirty-four moves both chains eight links, one module.

29
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:52,403
As the carriage arrives, a fixed cam draws its latch pin back, so the module is free to tilt inward.

30
00:02:52,403 --> 00:02:59,016
Its T-bracket slides straight into the electronics carriage's rail, whose flared ends only line it up.

31
00:02:59,016 --> 00:03:08,740
And a ramp at the front of the electronics carriage catches the door rod's head and draws it six millimetres, which slides the shutter off the outlet.

32
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:21,580
The auger's forty-four tooth gear pokes up through the carriage's opening, and the electronics carriage holds a twenty-four tooth steel pinion on its NEMA eleven motor just above it.

33
00:03:21,580 --> 00:03:23,745
Nothing presses the two together.

34
00:03:23,745 --> 00:03:38,112
The thirty-four and a quarter millimetres between their axes is set by where two separate parts sit: the module, on its hinge pins and latch shelf, and the electronics carriage, on its guide seat and the actuator's pin.

35
00:03:38,112 --> 00:03:49,331
During every index the motor's driver is off, so the pinion is free: as the gear slides in from the side, its teeth roll the pinion round about fifty degrees, like a rack.

36
00:03:49,331 --> 00:03:53,726
A MuJoCo model with real tooth contact shows where this is fragile.

37
00:03:53,726 --> 00:03:58,712
In about one index in four, a gear tooth lands on the tip of a pinion tooth.

38
00:03:58,712 --> 00:04:05,075
Nothing in version eleven can give, so the chain drives the two tips together and the index jams.

39
00:04:05,075 --> 00:04:18,983
The model also shows that the centre distance has only a seven tenths of a millimetre window: below thirty-four millimetres the teeth bind, and above thirty-four point seven two the contact ratio drops below one.

40
00:04:19,883 --> 00:04:29,206
When the actuator pulls, the electronics carriage lifts off its seat, and after two tenths of a millimetre the lips under the T's head pick up the module.

41
00:04:29,206 --> 00:04:35,623
From then on the module, the T, the electronics carriage and the pinion turn about the hinge as one piece.

42
00:04:35,623 --> 00:04:44,704
The teeth's own separating force pushes the gear away from the pinion, but the T's head is already resting on the lips, so the gear has nowhere to go.

43
00:04:44,704 --> 00:04:53,663
That captured T is what replaces gravity, and it works the same at ninety degrees, where the module's weight acts along the auger and helps nothing.

44
00:04:53,663 --> 00:05:00,504
In the model, no tooth jumped in any tilt and dose cycle, at thirty or ninety degrees, with the solenoid tapping.

45
00:05:00,504 --> 00:05:06,013
When the auger's load was ramped up, the motor stalled before any tooth jumped, every time.

46
00:05:06,013 --> 00:05:09,948
The actuator needed under thirty newtons, well inside its rating.

47
00:05:10,848 --> 00:05:21,138
The longer carriages put the hinge seventy-three millimetres behind the front chain, so the module and the electronics carriage can swing all the way to ninety degrees.

48
00:05:21,138 --> 00:05:25,426
One Actuonix P16, with a hundred and fifty millimetre stroke, does it.

49
00:05:25,426 --> 00:05:31,428
It runs through a slot in the front side plate to a clevis under a new bar across the front posts.

50
00:05:31,428 --> 00:05:40,739
It is a hundred and thirty-nine millimetres out at rest and eight at ninety degrees, and its lever about the hinge never drops below thirty millimetres.

51
00:05:41,639 --> 00:05:47,663
The solenoid now sits over clip one, in the first third of the auger, where taps move powder best.

52
00:05:47,663 --> 00:06:01,495
The outlet stays on the hinge axis at every tilt, so the dose always falls in the same place: thirty-three millimetres down, through a hole in a dosing lid set into the balance's draft cage, into a beaker on a printed pillar.

53
00:06:01,495 --> 00:06:14,159
After a dose the station holds for half a second, tilts back, sets the module on its shelf, and turns the auger back one revolution at rest, which the leakage study found clears the outlet without spilling.

54
00:06:15,059 --> 00:06:33,584
Every pose is checked for interference with exact solid booleans, counting anything over half a cubic millimetre: all eight hundred and twenty-nine parts at home, the tilt from zero to ninety degrees, every module passing the station, the latch cam as carriages pass it, and the swap at the side hatch.

55
00:06:33,584 --> 00:06:35,853
This round found three real problems.

56
00:06:35,853 --> 00:06:44,134
The longer version eleven carriages no longer came out whole through the side hatch, so the hatch is now four hundred millimetres wide.

57
00:06:44,134 --> 00:06:55,666
And two clashes had been hidden because the check skipped those pairs of parts: the electronics carriage's spine ran through the pinion, and the guide's seat sat inside the carriage's arm.

58
00:06:55,666 --> 00:07:00,757
Both are fixed, those pairs are checked again, and every check now comes out clean.

59
00:07:01,657 --> 00:07:14,277
Every printed part is sliced in Bambu Studio, in PETG at two tenths of a millimetre layers, with the three hundred and ninety-one millimetre carriage laid diagonally on the H2D's bed, on supports.

60
00:07:14,277 --> 00:07:28,571
Slicing found two parts that came apart into separate solids in the CAD, the electronics carriage and the outlet door's housing, plus a T-bracket whose head floated and a beaker pillar twice the weight the parts list said.

61
00:07:28,571 --> 00:07:29,794
All four are fixed.

62
00:07:29,794 --> 00:07:40,482
The vendors' pages were read through the lab's Raspberry Pi this time, in a real browser with mouse movements, because McMaster and Amazon block datacenter addresses.

63
00:07:40,482 --> 00:07:51,235
That confirmed the balance's height from A and D's manual, measured the chain plates from McMaster's own CAD, and replaced the estimated prices with real part numbers.

64
00:07:51,235 --> 00:07:58,833
The pinion, for one, only comes with a six millimetre bore, so it needs a sleeve on the motor's five millimetre shaft.

65
00:07:59,733 --> 00:08:03,411
The simulations point to one change above all: a spring.

66
00:08:03,411 --> 00:08:15,826
Put the NEMA eleven and its pinion on a small carrier that pivots in the electronics carriage, with a spring pressing the pinion into the gear with about six newtons, past a set-screw stop.

67
00:08:15,826 --> 00:08:20,227
Then the pinion always sits in tight mesh, whatever the tolerances.

68
00:08:20,227 --> 00:08:29,424
A tooth landing tip on tip lifts it instead of jamming, and a slow turn of the motor after the index, reversing once, drops it into the gap.

69
00:08:29,424 --> 00:08:40,131
In the model that took the jams from about one index in four to none, in every one of several hundred simulated indexes, over a millimetre and a half of tolerance.

70
00:08:40,131 --> 00:08:47,685
The preload stays above the mesh's separating force at the motor's stall, so the teeth stay engaged while it doses.

71
00:08:47,685 --> 00:08:53,400
It isn't in version eleven's CAD; it is the first change proposed for the next version.

72
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:54,714
Thanks for watching.
